  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Thursday)\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nA cold front will be working its way south through the forecast \narea at the start of the long-term. Showers and isolated \nthunderstorms will be on ongoing, with the heaviest rain axis from\nsoutheast OK through far northwest AR. This general area will \nremain the focus for showers and thunderstorms into the afternoon,\nnear and ahead of the southward advancing cold front that is \nforecast to slide south of Red River by late Friday afternoon or \nearly evening. Moderate to heavy rainfall will continue to be the \nbiggest threat through the daytime Friday, especially across \nsoutheast OK and western AR. Another half an inch to an inch of \nrain will be possible from Friday morning through Friday evening \nfor this area. \n\nThe mid-level trough shifts east of the area late Friday night, \nbut its axis will linger and hover over the area Saturday and \ninto Sunday morning. As overrunning occurs near the surface and \nthe trough axis remaining present overhead, this will maintain \nlow to medium rain chances (20-60%), mostly across southeast OK \nand west-central AR. Rainfall amounts are forecast to stay light \nSaturday into Sunday, generally up to quarter to half an inch\npossible. Rain chances completely end for all areas on or by \nSunday morning and will remain dry through the rest of the long-\nterm period. \n\nEnsembles and global deterministic models are in very good \nagreement that broad continental surface high pressure builds \ninto the Plains beginning on Sunday. As this occurs, mid-level \nridging will build in over the Great Basin/Rockies region. This \nwill ensure a dry and pleasant regime between Sunday and \nWednesday.\n\nTemperatures over the weekend will feature crisp fall mornings \nwith lows in the lower to middle 50s (perhaps dipping into the \nupper 40s in spots) and afternoon high temperatures in the low to\nmiddle 70s (with some lower 80s creeping into southeast OK on \nSunday). Temperatures will steadily trend warmer through the \ninto next week, underneath mostly sunny skies during the daytime.\nSoutherly winds are expected to return Monday night into Tuesday \nand temperatures will eventually trend back up into the low to \nmiddle 80s for highs by Wednesday.\n\nMejia",
